export const MATHS = [{"id":"WN-001","lv":"gr","d":2,"area":"Grapes","q":"Which grape smells of blackcurrant and cedar, with firm tannin, and anchors the Médoc?","o":["Tempranillo","Pinot Noir","Cabernet Sauvignon","Cabernet Franc"],"a":[2],"s":1,"x":"Thick-skinned and late-ripening, it needs warmth: Left Bank Bordeaux, Napa, Coonawarra, Margaret River.","fact":"Cabernet Sauvignon is the backbone of the Médoc and Napa: blackcurrant, cedar, firm tannin."},{"id":"WN-002","lv":"gr","d":2,"area":"Grapes","q":"Which grape dominates Pomerol and Saint-Émilion?","o":["Merlot","Cabernet Sauvignon","Malbec","Syrah"],"a":[0],"s":1,"x":"The Right Bank's clay suits early-ripening Merlot; Pétrus is almost pure Merlot.","fact":"Merlot is Cabernet's softer, plummy partner and leads on Bordeaux's Right Bank."},{"id":"WN-003","lv":"gr","d":1,"area":"Grapes","q":"Red Burgundy is made from","o":["Merlot","Gamay","Pinot Noir","Syrah"],"a":[2],"s":1,"x":"Thin-skinned and fussy, it wants cool climates: Burgundy, Champagne, Oregon, Central Otago, Germany (Spätburgunder).","fact":"Red Burgundy is Pinot Noir: pale, high in acid, red-fruited and earthy."},{"id":"WN-004","lv":"gr","d":2,"area":"Grapes","q":"Shiraz is the Australian name for","o":["Grenache","Syrah","Mourvèdre","Malbec"],"a":[1],"s":1,"x":"Same vine; the name signals the style: Syrah for the cooler, peppery one, Shiraz for the ripe, warm one.","fact":"Syrah and Shiraz are the same grape: peppery in the Rhône, jammy in the Barossa."},{"id":"WN-005","lv":"gr","d":3,"area":"Grapes","q":"Barolo is made from","o":["Nebbiolo","Dolcetto","Barbera","Sangiovese"],"a":[0],"s":1,"x":"Named for the autumn fog (nebbia) of the Langhe in Piedmont.","fact":"Nebbiolo makes Barolo and Barbaresco: pale in the glass, high in tannin and acid, tar and roses."},{"id":"WN-006","lv":"gr","d":3,"area":"Grapes","q":"Brunello di Montalcino is 100%","o":["Nebbiolo","Merlot","Sangiovese","Montepulciano"],"a":[2],"s":1,"x":"Brunello is the local name for the Sangiovese grown around Montalcino.","fact":"Sangiovese is Tuscany's grape: Chianti, Brunello di Montalcino, Vino Nobile di Montepulciano."},{"id":"WN-007","lv":"gr","d":2,"area":"Grapes","q":"Rioja's main red grape","o":["Tempranillo","Garnacha","Monastrell","Mencía"],"a":[0],"s":1,"x":"From temprano, early: it ripens early. In Portugal it's Tinta Roriz.","fact":"Tempranillo is Spain's great red: Rioja and Ribera del Duero."},{"id":"WN-008","lv":"gr","d":2,"area":"Grapes","q":"Which grape made Mendoza famous?","o":["Tannat","Malbec","Bonarda","Carménère"],"a":[1],"s":1,"x":"Cahors's 'black wine' is Malbec too; Mendoza's altitude keeps it fresh.","fact":"Malbec, once a Bordeaux and Cahors grape, became Argentina's signature."},{"id":"WN-009","lv":"gr","d":3,"area":"Grapes","q":"The main grape of Châteauneuf-du-Pape","o":["Syrah","Grenache","Carignan","Cinsault"],"a":[1],"s":1,"x":"Pale for its alcohol, strawberry and herbs; blended as GSM with Syrah and Mourvèdre.","fact":"Grenache (Garnacha) is the warm-climate red behind Châteauneuf-du-Pape and Priorat."},{"id":"WN-010","lv":"gr","d":4,"area":"Grapes","q":"Zinfandel is genetically identical to","o":["Barbera","Primitivo","Sangiovese","Grenache"],"a":[1],"s":1,"x":"DNA traced both to Crljenak Kaštelanski, from the Dalmatian coast.","fact":"Zinfandel and Italy's Primitivo are the same grape, originally from Croatia."},{"id":"WN-011","lv":"gr","d":1,"area":"Grapes","q":"Chablis is made from","o":["Sauvignon Blanc","Chardonnay","Chenin Blanc","Aligoté"],"a":[1],"s":1,"x":"A neutral grape that shows its place and its making: oak, malolactic and lees all mark it.","fact":"Chardonnay takes its style from the winemaker: steely in Chablis, buttery and oaky in Napa."},{"id":"WN-012","lv":"gr","d":2,"area":"Grapes","q":"Which grape makes Sancerre and Pouilly-Fumé?","o":["Melon de Bourgogne","Chenin Blanc","Sauvignon Blanc","Chardonnay"],"a":[2],"s":1,"x":"In the Loire flinty and restrained; in Marlborough pungent with passion fruit.","fact":"Sauvignon Blanc: gooseberry, cut grass and high acid; Sancerre and Marlborough."},{"id":"WN-013","lv":"gr","d":3,"area":"Grapes","q":"Which grape develops a 'petrol' note with age?","o":["Sémillon","Chardonnay","Viognier","Riesling"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"The compound is TDN, prized in old Riesling and encouraged by sun on the grapes.","fact":"Riesling has the highest acidity of the great whites and ages for decades; old, it smells of petrol."},{"id":"WN-014","lv":"gr","d":5,"area":"Grapes","q":"In South Africa, Chenin Blanc was traditionally called","o":["Colombard","Pinotage","Steen","Hanepoot"],"a":[2],"s":1,"x":"Dry, sweet or sparkling; quince, honey and wool.","fact":"Chenin Blanc is the Loire's chameleon (Vouvray, Savennières) and South Africa's most planted grape."},{"id":"WN-015","lv":"gr","d":3,"area":"Grapes","q":"Lychee and rose petal on the nose most likely mean","o":["Pinot Gris","Muscadet","Gewürztraminer","Riesling"],"a":[2],"s":1,"x":"Gewürz is German for spice; the skins are pink, so the wine is often deep gold.","fact":"Gewürztraminer smells of lychee and rose; low acid, high alcohol, Alsace's most aromatic."},{"id":"WN-016","lv":"gr","d":4,"area":"Grapes","q":"Condrieu is made from","o":["Marsanne","Chardonnay","Viognier","Roussanne"],"a":[2],"s":1,"x":"Nearly extinct in the 1960s; Côte-Rôtie may co-ferment up to 20% of it with Syrah.","fact":"Viognier: apricot and honeysuckle. Condrieu is pure Viognier, and a dash goes into Côte-Rôtie."},{"id":"WN-017","lv":"gr","d":4,"area":"Grapes","q":"White pepper in a white wine points to","o":["Chasselas","Sauvignon Blanc","Albariño","Grüner Veltliner"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"The pepper is rotundone, the same compound as in cool-climate Syrah.","fact":"Grüner Veltliner is Austria's grape, known for a white-pepper note."},{"id":"WN-018","lv":"gr","d":2,"area":"Grapes","q":"Beaujolais is made from","o":["Merlot","Pinot Noir","Gamay","Cinsault"],"a":[2],"s":1,"x":"Philip the Bold banned it from the Côte d'Or in 1395 as 'very bad and disloyal'.","fact":"Gamay is Beaujolais's grape: light, juicy, low in tannin."},{"id":"WN-019","lv":"bx","d":2,"area":"Bordeaux","q":"Which is a Left Bank appellation?","o":["Fronsac","Pauillac","Saint-Émilion","Pomerol"],"a":[1],"s":1,"x":"Pauillac holds three first growths: Lafite, Latour and Mouton.","fact":"The Left Bank (Médoc, Graves) is Cabernet on gravel; the Right Bank (Saint-Émilion, Pomerol) is Merlot on clay and limestone."},{"id":"WN-020","lv":"bx","d":3,"area":"Bordeaux","q":"The 1855 classification was drawn up for","o":["Napoleon's coronation","the Treaty of Paris","the Paris Exposition Universelle","the founding of the appellation system"],"a":[2],"s":1,"x":"Brokers ranked the châteaux by price; the list has changed once since.","fact":"The 1855 Classification ranked the Médoc's reds into five growths, for the Paris Exposition Universelle."},{"id":"WN-021","lv":"bx","d":3,"area":"Bordeaux","q":"Which is NOT a first growth?","o":["Haut-Brion","Pétrus","Margaux","Latour"],"a":[1],"s":1,"x":"Pétrus is in Pomerol, which has never been classified.","fact":"The five first growths: Lafite Rothschild, Latour, Margaux, Haut-Brion and Mouton Rothschild."},{"id":"WN-022","lv":"bx","d":4,"area":"Bordeaux","q":"Which château was promoted to first growth in 1973?","o":["Cheval Blanc","Lynch-Bages","Mouton Rothschild","Lafite Rothschild"],"a":[2],"s":1,"x":"After decades of lobbying by Baron Philippe; its labels are commissioned from artists each year: Picasso, Warhol, Bacon.","fact":"Mouton Rothschild was promoted to first growth in 1973, the only change to the 1855 list."},{"id":"WN-023","lv":"bx","d":4,"area":"Bordeaux","q":"The first growth outside the Médoc is","o":["Lafite Rothschild","Haut-Brion","Latour","Margaux"],"a":[1],"s":1,"x":"Samuel Pepys drank 'Ho Bryan' in London in 1663: one of the first wines known by name.","fact":"Haut-Brion is the one first growth outside the Médoc, in Pessac-Léognan (Graves)."},{"id":"WN-024","lv":"bx","d":3,"area":"Bordeaux","q":"Pétrus is mostly","o":["Cabernet Franc","Cabernet Sauvignon","Malbec","Merlot"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"It sits on a buttonhole of blue clay that suits Merlot.","fact":"Pomerol has no classification, yet holds Pétrus and Le Pin, among the most expensive wines in the world."},{"id":"WN-025","lv":"bx","d":8,"area":"Bordeaux","q":"Which two châteaux make up Saint-Émilion's 'Premier Grand Cru Classé A' since 2022?","o":["Pavie and Figeac","Pétrus and Le Pin","Ausone and Cheval Blanc","Angélus and Ausone"],"a":[0],"s":1,"x":"The three left in protest at rules that weighed marketing and visits; Pétrus and Le Pin are in Pomerol.","fact":"Saint-Émilion's classification is revised about every ten years; since 2022 its top tier (A) is Pavie and Figeac, after Ausone, Cheval Blanc and Angélus withdrew."},{"id":"WN-026","lv":"bx","d":4,"area":"Bordeaux","q":"Château d'Yquem's rank in 1855 was","o":["Premier Cru","Hors Classe","Premier Cru Supérieur","Premier Grand Cru Classé A"],"a":[2],"s":1,"x":"A rank created for it alone.","fact":"Sauternes is sweet, from botrytised Sémillon and Sauvignon Blanc; Château d'Yquem is its sole 'Premier Cru Supérieur'."},{"id":"WN-027","lv":"bx","d":3,"area":"Bordeaux","q":"Which grape is NOT a traditional Bordeaux red variety?","o":["Syrah","Cabernet Franc","Malbec","Petit Verdot"],"a":[0],"s":1,"x":"Carménère is permitted too, a relic of pre-phylloxera Bordeaux.","fact":"Bordeaux reds blend Cabernet Sauvignon, Merlot, Cabernet Franc, Petit Verdot and Malbec."},{"id":"WN-028","lv":"bx","d":4,"area":"Bordeaux","q":"Les Forts de Latour is","o":["Latour's second wine","a Sauternes","a Pomerol","a white Graves"],"a":[0],"s":1,"x":"A way into a great estate's style at a fraction of the price.","fact":"Top châteaux sell a second wine from younger vines or set-aside lots: Carruades de Lafite, Les Forts de Latour, Pavillon Rouge."},{"id":"WN-029","lv":"bx","d":5,"area":"Bordeaux","q":"Bordeaux's best dry whites come from","o":["Margaux","Pomerol","Pauillac","Pessac-Léognan"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"Sauvignon Blanc and Sémillon, often barrel-fermented: Haut-Brion Blanc, Domaine de Chevalier.","fact":"Pessac-Léognan was carved out of the northern Graves in 1987 and makes Bordeaux's best dry whites."},{"id":"WN-030","lv":"bx","d":5,"area":"Bordeaux","q":"Château Palmer is in","o":["Pauillac","Saint-Julien","Pomerol","Margaux"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"Margaux is a commune, an appellation and a first growth: Château Margaux.","fact":"Château Palmer, a third growth that often sells at second-growth prices, is in the Margaux appellation."},{"id":"WN-031","lv":"bg","d":2,"area":"Burgundy & Beaujolais","q":"Burgundy's top tier is","o":["Grand Cru","Premier Cru","Cru Bourgeois","Grand Cru Classé"],"a":[0],"s":1,"x":"Unlike Bordeaux, Burgundy classifies vineyards, not producers.","fact":"Burgundy rises from regional (Bourgogne) to village to Premier Cru to Grand Cru, about 1% of production."},{"id":"WN-032","lv":"bg","d":3,"area":"Burgundy & Beaujolais","q":"Puligny-Montrachet and Meursault are in","o":["the Côte de Beaune","the Côte de Nuits","the Mâconnais","Chablis"],"a":[0],"s":1,"x":"Montrachet, the greatest white Grand Cru, straddles Puligny and Chassagne.","fact":"The Côte d'Or splits into the Côte de Nuits (north, mostly great reds) and the Côte de Beaune (south, the great whites)."},{"id":"WN-033","lv":"bg","d":4,"area":"Burgundy & Beaujolais","q":"La Romanée-Conti is in","o":["Vosne-Romanée","Chablis","Gevrey-Chambertin","Pommard"],"a":[0],"s":1,"x":"DRC also owns La Tâche outright, and parts of Richebourg, Romanée-Saint-Vivant and Montrachet.","fact":"La Romanée-Conti, a 1.8-hectare monopole of the Domaine de la Romanée-Conti in Vosne-Romanée, is one of the most expensive wines on earth."},{"id":"WN-034","lv":"bg","d":4,"area":"Burgundy & Beaujolais","q":"Chablis's famous soil is","o":["granite","volcanic basalt","slate","Kimmeridgian limestone"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"Hence the oyster-shell, saline note, and the classic match with oysters.","fact":"Chablis grows on Kimmeridgian limestone, full of fossil oyster shells; its seven Grand Cru climats share one slope over the Serein."},{"id":"WN-035","lv":"bg","d":3,"area":"Burgundy & Beaujolais","q":"Pouilly-Fuissé is","o":["Chardonnay from southern Burgundy","a red from the Rhône","Chenin Blanc from the Loire","Sauvignon Blanc from the Loire"],"a":[0],"s":1,"x":"The most common mix-up on a French wine list.","fact":"Pouilly-Fuissé is Chardonnay from the Mâconnais; Pouilly-Fumé is Sauvignon Blanc from the Loire."},{"id":"WN-036","lv":"bg","d":5,"area":"Burgundy & Beaujolais","q":"In Burgundy, a 'climat' is","o":["a cellar","a named vineyard plot","the weather of a vintage","a cooperative"],"a":[1],"s":1,"x":"Over a thousand of them, mapped by monks since the Middle Ages.","fact":"Burgundy's climats, its named vineyard plots, became a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 2015."},{"id":"WN-037","lv":"bg","d":5,"area":"Burgundy & Beaujolais","q":"The only red Grand Cru in the Côte de Beaune","o":["Volnay","Corton","Montrachet","Pommard"],"a":[1],"s":1,"x":"Charlemagne is said to have owned vines on the hill.","fact":"Corton is the Côte de Beaune's only red Grand Cru; Corton-Charlemagne is white."},{"id":"WN-038","lv":"bg","d":3,"area":"Burgundy & Beaujolais","q":"Which is a Beaujolais cru?","o":["Morgon","Irancy","Mâcon","Givry"],"a":[0],"s":1,"x":"The others: Brouilly, Côte de Brouilly, Chénas, Chiroubles, Juliénas, Régnié, Saint-Amour.","fact":"Beaujolais has ten crus, among them Morgon, Fleurie and Moulin-à-Vent; their labels often leave out 'Beaujolais'."},{"id":"WN-039","lv":"bg","d":2,"area":"Burgundy & Beaujolais","q":"Beaujolais Nouveau is released","o":["on the second Tuesday of October","on the last Friday of September","on the third Thursday of November","on the first Sunday of December"],"a":[2],"s":1,"x":"Made by carbonic maceration to be drunk at once.","fact":"Beaujolais Nouveau is released on the third Thursday of November, weeks after the harvest."},{"id":"WN-040","lv":"bg","d":5,"area":"Burgundy & Beaujolais","q":"The Hospices de Beaune is","o":["a grape variety","a cellar museum","a cooperative","a charity wine auction"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"The hospital, founded in 1443, owns vineyards given by benefactors.","fact":"The Hospices de Beaune charity auction, held every November since 1859, sets the tone for the Burgundy vintage."},{"id":"WN-041","lv":"bg","d":5,"area":"Burgundy & Beaujolais","q":"In Burgundy, a monopole is","o":["a vineyard classed as a Grand Cru on its own","a vineyard sold to a single merchant","a vineyard owned by a single domaine","a vineyard planted with a single grape"],"a":[2],"s":1,"x":"Most Burgundy vineyards are split among many owners.","fact":"A monopole is a vineyard owned entirely by one domaine: La Tâche, La Romanée-Conti, Clos de Tart."},{"id":"WN-042","lv":"bg","d":4,"area":"Burgundy & Beaujolais","q":"Napoleon's favourite wine, Chambertin, comes from","o":["Gevrey-Chambertin","Chambolle-Musigny","Vosne-Romanée","Nuits-Saint-Georges"],"a":[0],"s":1,"x":"He reputedly drank it diluted with water, even on campaign.","fact":"Napoleon's favourite wine was Chambertin, a Grand Cru in Gevrey-Chambertin."},{"id":"WN-043","lv":"ch","d":2,"area":"Champagne & sparkling","q":"Which grape is NOT one of Champagne's three main varieties?","o":["Pinot Noir","Chardonnay","Meunier","Sauvignon Blanc"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"Four rare others are permitted, Arbane and Petit Meslier among them.","fact":"Champagne is made from Chardonnay, Pinot Noir and Pinot Meunier."},{"id":"WN-044","lv":"ch","d":3,"area":"Champagne & sparkling","q":"Where does the second fermentation happen in the traditional method?","o":["in the vat, with CO2 added","in oak barrels","in the bottle","in a pressurised tank"],"a":[2],"s":1,"x":"The dead yeast (lees) left in the bottle gives the bread and brioche.","fact":"The traditional method: a second fermentation in the bottle makes the bubbles, then riddling, disgorging and dosage."},{"id":"WN-045","lv":"ch","d":3,"area":"Champagne & sparkling","q":"Which is sweeter, Brut or Extra Dry?","o":["Extra Dry","they're the same","it depends on the grape","Brut"],"a":[0],"s":1,"x":"Brut is under 12 g of sugar a litre; Extra Dry 12 to 17.","fact":"From driest: Brut Nature, Extra Brut, Brut, Extra Dry, Sec, Demi-Sec, Doux. Extra Dry is sweeter than Brut."},{"id":"WN-046","lv":"ch","d":3,"area":"Champagne & sparkling","q":"Blanc de Noirs Champagne is made from","o":["white grapes (Chardonnay only)","a blend with still red wine added","red grapes left on their skins for colour","black grapes (Pinot Noir, Meunier)"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"Chardonnay only is Blanc de Blancs. The juice of black grapes is clear; pressed gently, it stays white.","fact":"Blanc de Blancs is white from white grapes (Chardonnay); Blanc de Noirs is white from black grapes."},{"id":"WN-047","lv":"ch","d":3,"area":"Champagne & sparkling","q":"Non-vintage Champagne is","o":["a blend of several years","unaged","the wine of a bad year","from a single vineyard"],"a":[0],"s":1,"x":"Non-vintage must age at least 15 months, vintage at least three years.","fact":"Most Champagne is non-vintage, a blend of years for a steady house style; a vintage is made only in good years."},{"id":"WN-048","lv":"ch","d":3,"area":"Champagne & sparkling","q":"Dom Pérignon is made by","o":["Louis Roederer","Krug","Veuve Clicquot","Moët & Chandon"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"The monk didn't invent sparkling Champagne, though he refined blending and pressing.","fact":"Dom Pérignon is Moët & Chandon's prestige cuvée, named after the 17th-century Benedictine monk."},{"id":"WN-049","lv":"ch","d":4,"area":"Champagne & sparkling","q":"Cristal was first made for","o":["Queen Victoria","Tsar Alexander II","Louis XIV","Napoleon III"],"a":[1],"s":1,"x":"The clear glass, so the story goes, so no bomb could hide in it.","fact":"Cristal, Louis Roederer's prestige cuvée, was first made in 1876 for Tsar Alexander II, in a clear bottle."},{"id":"WN-050","lv":"ch","d":5,"area":"Champagne & sparkling","q":"Riddling was invented at","o":["Veuve Clicquot","Moët & Chandon","Krug","Dom Pérignon's abbey"],"a":[0],"s":1,"x":"Barbe-Nicole Ponsardin, the widow (veuve) Clicquot, ran the house from 1805.","fact":"Riddling (remuage) was invented at Veuve Clicquot around 1816, to clear the sediment."},{"id":"WN-051","lv":"ch","d":2,"area":"Champagne & sparkling","q":"Prosecco's bubbles come from","o":["a second fermentation in a tank","added CO2","a second fermentation in the bottle","carbonic maceration"],"a":[0],"s":1,"x":"Cheaper and quicker; the fruit stays at the front.","fact":"Prosecco is made from Glera by the tank (Charmat) method: fruity and fresh rather than yeasty."},{"id":"WN-052","lv":"ch","d":3,"area":"Champagne & sparkling","q":"Which sparkling wine is made the same way as Champagne?","o":["Prosecco","Lambrusco","Moscato d'Asti","Cava"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"So do Franciacorta in Italy and England's sparkling wines.","fact":"Cava (Spain) and Crémant (France outside Champagne) use the traditional method."},{"id":"WN-053","lv":"ch","d":4,"area":"Champagne & sparkling","q":"Why does southern England suit sparkling wine?","o":["volcanic soils and a hot climate like Etna's","granite soils and a dry climate like the Douro's","slate soils and a steep climate like the Mosel's","chalk soils and a cool climate like Champagne's"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"English sparkling wines now win blind tastings against Champagne.","fact":"Southern England's chalk is the same seam as Champagne's; Taittinger and Pommery have both invested there."},{"id":"WN-054","lv":"ch","d":2,"area":"Champagne & sparkling","q":"A magnum holds","o":["3 litres","6 litres","1.5 litres","1 litre"],"a":[2],"s":1,"x":"Wine ages more slowly and more gracefully in big bottles.","fact":"A magnum is 1.5 litres, two bottles; in Champagne a Jeroboam is 3 litres and a Nebuchadnezzar 15."},{"id":"WN-055","lv":"ch","d":5,"area":"Champagne & sparkling","q":"The pressure in a bottle of Champagne is about","o":["1.5 atmospheres","6 atmospheres","60 atmospheres","20 atmospheres"],"a":[1],"s":1,"x":"Hence the thick glass, the punt and the wire cage (muselet).","fact":"A bottle of Champagne holds about 6 atmospheres, two to three times a car tyre."},{"id":"WN-056","lv":"fr","d":3,"area":"Rhône, Loire, Alsace, Provence","q":"Red Hermitage is made from","o":["Grenache","Pinot Noir","Merlot","Syrah"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"Black pepper, olive and violet on steep granite.","fact":"Northern Rhône reds are Syrah: Côte-Rôtie, Hermitage, Cornas, Saint-Joseph, Crozes-Hermitage."},{"id":"WN-057","lv":"fr","d":3,"area":"Rhône, Loire, Alsace, Provence","q":"Châteauneuf-du-Pape is named after","o":["the popes of Avignon","a castle of the Dukes of Burgundy","Napoleon","a Roman camp"],"a":[0],"s":1,"x":"The 'new castle of the pope' was their summer residence in the 14th century.","fact":"Châteauneuf-du-Pape takes its name from the Avignon popes and allows 13 grape varieties, Grenache leading."},{"id":"WN-058","lv":"fr","d":5,"area":"Rhône, Loire, Alsace, Provence","q":"The galets roulés of Châteauneuf-du-Pape are","o":["round stones that store the day's heat","round barrels that age the wine faster","round bottles embossed with the papal keys","round bales of straw that dry the grapes"],"a":[0],"s":1,"x":"Left by an ancient course of the Rhône.","fact":"Châteauneuf-du-Pape's galets roulés, large round stones, store the day's heat and give it back at night."},{"id":"WN-059","lv":"fr","d":4,"area":"Rhône, Loire, Alsace, Provence","q":"Tavel makes only","o":["sweet white","red","rosé","sparkling wine"],"a":[2],"s":1,"x":"Darker and fuller than Provence's pale rosés.","fact":"Tavel, in the southern Rhône, makes only rosé: deep, dry and Grenache-based."},{"id":"WN-060","lv":"fr","d":3,"area":"Rhône, Loire, Alsace, Provence","q":"'Sur lie' on a Muscadet label means","o":["organic","aged on its yeast lees","from old vines","unfiltered and sweet"],"a":[1],"s":1,"x":"The classic match for oysters on the Atlantic coast.","fact":"Muscadet, from Melon de Bourgogne near Nantes, is aged sur lie, on its yeast, for texture."},{"id":"WN-061","lv":"fr","d":4,"area":"Rhône, Loire, Alsace, Provence","q":"Chinon red is","o":["Gamay","Cabernet Franc","Pinot Noir","Malbec"],"a":[1],"s":1,"x":"Raspberry, red pepper and pencil lead, served cool.","fact":"The Loire's great reds, Chinon and Bourgueil, are Cabernet Franc."},{"id":"WN-062","lv":"fr","d":3,"area":"Rhône, Loire, Alsace, Provence","q":"What's unusual about Alsace labels, for France?","o":["they leave out the vintage","they name the grape","they're in German","they name the château"],"a":[1],"s":1,"x":"Riesling, Gewürztraminer, Pinot Gris and Muscat are its noble grapes.","fact":"Alsace labels its wines by grape, unusual in France, in tall flute bottles."},{"id":"WN-063","lv":"fr","d":5,"area":"Rhône, Loire, Alsace, Provence","q":"Sélection de Grains Nobles means","o":["vineyards of noble families","a selection of botrytised berries","a Grand Cru","aged in new oak"],"a":[1],"s":1,"x":"Alsace's answer to Germany's Beerenauslese.","fact":"Sélection de Grains Nobles (botrytised berries) and Vendanges Tardives (late harvest) are Alsace's sweet wines."},{"id":"WN-064","lv":"fr","d":4,"area":"Rhône, Loire, Alsace, Provence","q":"Bandol's reds are based on","o":["Mourvèdre","Grenache","Carignan","Syrah"],"a":[0],"s":1,"x":"At least half Mourvèdre, which needs the Mediterranean heat to ripen.","fact":"Provence makes the pale, dry rosé that conquered the world; Bandol makes long-lived reds from Mourvèdre."},{"id":"WN-065","lv":"it","d":3,"area":"Italy","q":"Italy's highest appellation category","o":["DOCG","IGT","Vino da Tavola","DOC"],"a":[0],"s":1,"x":"Denominazione di Origine Controllata e Garantita: 'guaranteed' by a tasting panel.","fact":"DOCG is Italy's top appellation tier, above DOC and IGT."},{"id":"WN-066","lv":"it","d":3,"area":"Italy","q":"Barolo and Barbaresco come from","o":["Piedmont","Tuscany","Sicily","Veneto"],"a":[0],"s":1,"x":"Barolo, 'the king of wines and the wine of kings'.","fact":"Barolo must age 38 months (18 in wood), Barbaresco 26: both Nebbiolo, both in Piedmont's Langhe."},{"id":"WN-067","lv":"it","d":4,"area":"Italy","q":"A black rooster on the neck of a bottle marks","o":["Brunello di Montalcino","Barolo","Chianti Classico","Prosecco"],"a":[2],"s":1,"x":"Legend has it the border with Siena was settled by a race between two roosters.","fact":"Chianti Classico bottles carry the black rooster (Gallo Nero), the old emblem of the Chianti League."},{"id":"WN-068","lv":"it","d":4,"area":"Italy","q":"Sassicaia is mainly","o":["Sangiovese Grosso","Montepulciano","Merlot and Syrah","Cabernet Sauvignon"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"Tenuta San Guido in Bolgheri; first released in 1971, now its own DOC.","fact":"Super Tuscans broke the rules in the 1970s with Cabernet and Merlot, first sold as humble table wine: Sassicaia, Tignanello, Ornellaia."},{"id":"WN-069","lv":"it","d":4,"area":"Italy","q":"Montepulciano d'Abruzzo is made from","o":["the Nebbiolo grape","the Montepulciano grape","the Sangiovese grape","the Aglianico grape"],"a":[1],"s":1,"x":"Vino Nobile di Montepulciano, the Tuscan town's wine, is Sangiovese. One of the classic traps on an Italian list.","fact":"Vino Nobile di Montepulciano is Sangiovese from the Tuscan town; Montepulciano d'Abruzzo is a different grape, from another region."},{"id":"WN-070","lv":"it","d":3,"area":"Italy","q":"Amarone's richness comes from","o":["drying the grapes before fermenting","freezing the grapes on the vine","adding brandy during fermenting","leaving noble rot on the grapes"],"a":[0],"s":1,"x":"Corvina from Valpolicella, near Verona; Recioto is its sweet sibling.","fact":"Amarone is made from grapes dried for months (appassimento): rich, dry and 15–16% alcohol."},{"id":"WN-071","lv":"it","d":4,"area":"Italy","q":"Soave's grape is","o":["Cortese","Garganega","Glera","Trebbiano"],"a":[1],"s":1,"x":"Gavi, its Piedmontese rival, is Cortese.","fact":"Soave is a white from Garganega, near Verona."},{"id":"WN-072","lv":"it","d":5,"area":"Italy","q":"Nerello Mascalese grows on","o":["Mount Etna","the Alps","Sardinia","Vesuvius"],"a":[0],"s":1,"x":"Old vines up to 1,000 metres, on lava.","fact":"Etna's volcanic slopes make fashionable reds from Nerello Mascalese."},{"id":"WN-073","lv":"it","d":3,"area":"Italy","q":"Moscato d'Asti is","o":["sweet, still and fortified with brandy","dry, lightly sparkling and red","sweet, lightly sparkling and low in alcohol","dry, fully sparkling and high in alcohol"],"a":[2],"s":1,"x":"The fermentation is stopped early to keep the grape's sugar.","fact":"Moscato d'Asti is sweet, lightly sparkling and only about 5.5% alcohol."},{"id":"WN-074","lv":"it","d":5,"area":"Italy","q":"Gaja is a name synonymous with","o":["Prosecco","Chianti","Barbaresco","Amarone"],"a":[2],"s":1,"x":"He also pioneered Cabernet and Chardonnay in Piedmont.","fact":"Angelo Gaja put Barbaresco on the world's tables from the 1960s."},{"id":"WN-075","lv":"ib","d":4,"area":"Spain & Portugal","q":"A red Rioja Gran Reserva ages at least","o":["2 years, 1 in oak","3 years, all in oak","10 years in bottle","5 years, 2 of them in oak"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"Rioja releases its wines ready to drink.","fact":"Rioja red: Crianza ages 2 years (1 in oak), Reserva 3 (1 in oak), Gran Reserva 5 (2 in oak)."},{"id":"WN-076","lv":"ib","d":4,"area":"Spain & Portugal","q":"Dill and coconut in a Rioja point to","o":["stainless steel","American oak","carbonic maceration","French oak"],"a":[1],"s":1,"x":"Modern producers use more French oak.","fact":"Traditional Rioja ages in American oak: vanilla, coconut and dill."},{"id":"WN-077","lv":"ib","d":4,"area":"Spain & Portugal","q":"Vega Sicilia is in","o":["Rioja Alavesa","Priorat in Catalonia","Toro in Zamora","Ribera del Duero"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"Pingus is Ribera's modern cult wine.","fact":"Vega Sicilia Único, from Ribera del Duero, is Spain's most famous wine, released about a decade after the vintage."},{"id":"WN-078","lv":"ib","d":6,"area":"Spain & Portugal","q":"Priorat's soil is","o":["sand","volcanic ash","llicorella slate","chalk"],"a":[2],"s":1,"x":"Steep, poor and Catalan, revived in the late 1980s.","fact":"Priorat's old Garnacha and Cariñena grow on llicorella, a black slate; it and Rioja are Spain's two top-tier DOCa."},{"id":"WN-079","lv":"ib","d":3,"area":"Spain & Portugal","q":"Albariño's home is","o":["Rías Baixas","Rioja","Jerez","La Mancha"],"a":[0],"s":1,"x":"Across the river in Portugal it's Alvarinho, in Vinho Verde.","fact":"Albariño from Rías Baixas in Galicia: fresh, peachy, saline, made for seafood."},{"id":"WN-080","lv":"ib","d":4,"area":"Spain & Portugal","q":"Fino sherry ages under","o":["a layer of yeast called flor","a layer of oil called the crown","a seal of wax called the cap","a layer of mould called botrytis"],"a":[0],"s":1,"x":"Serve it cold, like a white wine, with almonds and ham.","fact":"Fino and Manzanilla age under flor, a layer of yeast that shields them from air: pale, bone dry, salty."},{"id":"WN-081","lv":"ib","d":5,"area":"Spain & Portugal","q":"Which sherry ages entirely with air, without flor?","o":["Amontillado","Oloroso","Manzanilla","Fino"],"a":[1],"s":1,"x":"Dark, nutty and dry, despite its sweet reputation.","fact":"Amontillado begins under flor and then ages with air; Oloroso ages with air from the start."},{"id":"WN-082","lv":"ib","d":5,"area":"Spain & Portugal","q":"A solera is","o":["a single-vintage ageing system of barrels","a sun-drying system for the grapes","a cooling system for fermenting tanks","a fractional blending system of barrels"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"So sherry rarely carries a vintage.","fact":"Sherry ages in a solera: fractional blending, the oldest barrels topped up from younger ones."},{"id":"WN-083","lv":"ib","d":5,"area":"Spain & Portugal","q":"Manzanilla comes from","o":["El Puerto de Santa María","Sanlúcar de Barrameda","Seville","Jerez de la Frontera"],"a":[1],"s":1,"x":"Salty, light and the driest of all.","fact":"Manzanilla is Fino made by the sea, at Sanlúcar de Barrameda, where the flor grows thicker."},{"id":"WN-084","lv":"ib","d":3,"area":"Spain & Portugal","q":"Port is sweet because","o":["spirit is added after fermentation","sugar is added before bottling","the grapes are dried before pressing","spirit is added mid-fermentation"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"Sherry is fortified after fermentation, so it starts dry.","fact":"Port is fortified during fermentation with grape spirit, which stops it and keeps the sugar."},{"id":"WN-085","lv":"ib","d":4,"area":"Spain & Portugal","q":"A 20-year-old Tawny Port is","o":["made from 20 grapes","a single vintage, 20 years in bottle","bottled after 20 days","aged in wood, the 20 an indication of style"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"Vintage Port throws a sediment and needs decanting.","fact":"Vintage Port is declared only in great years and ages for decades in bottle; Tawny ages in wood and is labelled 10, 20, 30 or 40 years."},{"id":"WN-086","lv":"ib","d":5,"area":"Spain & Portugal","q":"Madeira's distinctive process is","o":["drying the grapes before pressing","heating the wine as it ages","freezing the wine as it ages","ageing the wine under flor"],"a":[1],"s":1,"x":"It copies the heat of the voyages through the tropics.","fact":"Madeira is heated as it ages, so it is almost indestructible once opened; from dry to sweet: Sercial, Verdelho, Bual, Malmsey."},{"id":"WN-087","lv":"ib","d":3,"area":"Spain & Portugal","q":"In Vinho Verde, 'verde' refers to","o":["unripe grapes","youth","the region's forests","the colour"],"a":[1],"s":1,"x":"From the Minho, in Portugal's green north.","fact":"Vinho Verde is young, light and often a little spritzy: 'green' means young, not the colour."},{"id":"WN-088","lv":"de","d":3,"area":"Germany, Austria, Switzerland","q":"The lightest Prädikat level is","o":["Kabinett","Trockenbeerenauslese","Spätlese","Auslese"],"a":[0],"s":1,"x":"The levels measure ripeness, not sweetness: a Spätlese can be dry.","fact":"German Prädikat levels rise with ripeness at harvest: Kabinett, Spätlese, Auslese, Beerenauslese, Trockenbeerenauslese, plus Eiswein."},{"id":"WN-089","lv":"de","d":3,"area":"Germany, Austria, Switzerland","q":"Spätlese means","o":["ice wine","selected bunches","late harvest","dry"],"a":[2],"s":1,"x":"Auslese means selected bunches.","fact":"Spätlese means late harvest."},{"id":"WN-090","lv":"de","d":4,"area":"Germany, Austria, Switzerland","q":"Trockenbeerenauslese is made from","o":["frozen berries, picked at night in the cold","late-picked bunches, fully ripe","selected ripe bunches, some botrytised","dried, botrytised berries, picked one by one"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"The others are Eiswein, Spätlese and Auslese. Tiny yields, huge acid, and decades of life.","fact":"Trockenbeerenauslese is made from shrivelled, botrytised berries picked one by one: among the sweetest wines on earth."},{"id":"WN-091","lv":"de","d":3,"area":"Germany, Austria, Switzerland","q":"'Trocken' means","o":["dry","sweet","red","old vines"],"a":[0],"s":1,"x":"Halbtrocken is off-dry.","fact":"'Trocken' on a German label means dry; Grosses Gewächs (GG) is the VDP's dry grand cru."},{"id":"WN-092","lv":"de","d":4,"area":"Germany, Austria, Switzerland","q":"The Mosel is famous for Riesling grown on","o":["chalk","slate","sand","clay"],"a":[1],"s":1,"x":"Some of Europe's steepest vineyards, worked by hand.","fact":"The Mosel's steep slate slopes make Germany's most delicate Rieslings, often 8 to 9% alcohol."},{"id":"WN-093","lv":"de","d":4,"area":"Germany, Austria, Switzerland","q":"Eiswein grapes are","o":["dried on mats","botrytised","picked early","frozen on the vine"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"The water stays behind as ice; the sugar and acid come out.","fact":"Eiswein is pressed from grapes frozen on the vine, at −7 °C or colder."},{"id":"WN-094","lv":"de","d":4,"area":"Germany, Austria, Switzerland","q":"Spätburgunder is German for","o":["Pinot Gris","Pinot Noir","Riesling","Gamay"],"a":[1],"s":1,"x":"Grauburgunder is Pinot Gris; Weissburgunder is Pinot Blanc.","fact":"Germany is one of the world's biggest Pinot Noir producers; there it's called Spätburgunder."},{"id":"WN-095","lv":"de","d":4,"area":"Germany, Austria, Switzerland","q":"Franken's traditional bottle is","o":["the Bocksbeutel","the clavelin","the flute","the fiasco"],"a":[0],"s":1,"x":"The Würzburger Stein is its most famous vineyard.","fact":"Franken, around Würzburg in Bavaria, is known for Silvaner in the flat, round Bocksbeutel bottle."},{"id":"WN-096","lv":"de","d":5,"area":"Germany, Austria, Switzerland","q":"Austria's 1985 wine scandal involved","o":["sulphuric acid","diethylene glycol","lead","methanol"],"a":[1],"s":1,"x":"Austrian wine rebuilt itself on quality, Grüner Veltliner leading.","fact":"In 1985 some Austrian producers were caught sweetening wine with diethylene glycol, an antifreeze ingredient; the scandal led to some of Europe's strictest wine laws."},{"id":"WN-097","lv":"de","d":6,"area":"Germany, Austria, Switzerland","q":"In the Wachau, the richest dry wines are called","o":["Steinfeder","Kabinett","Smaragd","Federspiel"],"a":[2],"s":1,"x":"Smaragd is the emerald lizard of its terraces.","fact":"The Wachau grades its dry wines by ripeness: Steinfeder (lightest), Federspiel, Smaragd (richest)."},{"id":"WN-098","lv":"de","d":3,"area":"Germany, Austria, Switzerland","q":"In the Valais, Chasselas is called","o":["Petite Arvine","Fendant","Amigne","Johannisberg"],"a":[1],"s":1,"x":"Johannisberg is the Valais name for Silvaner.","fact":"Chasselas is Switzerland's white grape; in the Valais it's called Fendant."},{"id":"WN-099","lv":"de","d":3,"area":"Germany, Austria, Switzerland","q":"Lavaux's vineyards overlook","o":["Lake Zurich","Lake Geneva","Lake Lucerne","Lake Constance"],"a":[1],"s":1,"x":"Three suns, they say: the sky, the lake and the stone walls.","fact":"Lavaux, the terraces above Lake Geneva between Lausanne and Montreux, has been a UNESCO World Heritage Site since 2007; Dézaley is its grand cru."},{"id":"WN-100","lv":"de","d":4,"area":"Germany, Austria, Switzerland","q":"Which is a native grape of the Valais?","o":["Grüner Veltliner","Albariño","Malbec","Petite Arvine"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"Dôle, a Pinot Noir and Gamay blend, is its everyday red.","fact":"The Valais is Switzerland's biggest wine canton; Petite Arvine (white, salty finish) and Cornalin (red) are its own grapes."},{"id":"WN-101","lv":"de","d":4,"area":"Germany, Austria, Switzerland","q":"Switzerland's largest wine-growing commune is","o":["Lutry","Neuchâtel","Sion","Satigny"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"Gamaret, a Swiss crossing of Gamay, was bred at Changins in 1970.","fact":"Geneva is Switzerland's third-largest wine canton, and Satigny its largest wine-growing commune; Chasselas and Gamay lead."},{"id":"WN-102","lv":"de","d":4,"area":"Germany, Austria, Switzerland","q":"How much Swiss wine is exported?","o":["about 1–2%","about 80%","about 20%","about half"],"a":[0],"s":1,"x":"Which is why it's barely known abroad.","fact":"Switzerland drinks almost all its own wine: only about 1% to 2% is exported."},{"id":"WN-103","lv":"de","d":4,"area":"Germany, Austria, Switzerland","q":"Switzerland's Merlot comes mostly from","o":["the Valais","Ticino","Vaud","Geneva"],"a":[1],"s":1,"x":"Planted after phylloxera, it now covers most of the canton's vineyards.","fact":"Ticino, the Italian-speaking south, makes Switzerland's Merlot."},{"id":"WN-104","lv":"nw","d":3,"area":"The New World","q":"The 1976 'Judgment of Paris' was won by wines from","o":["Italy","Chile","California","Australia"],"a":[2],"s":1,"x":"Organised by the English merchant Steven Spurrier; it made Napa.","fact":"In the 1976 Judgment of Paris, Californian wines beat top French ones in a blind tasting: Stag's Leap's Cabernet and Chateau Montelena's Chardonnay."},{"id":"WN-105","lv":"nw","d":2,"area":"The New World","q":"Napa Valley's signature grape","o":["Cabernet Franc","Petite Sirah","Zinfandel","Cabernet Sauvignon"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"Screaming Eagle and Harlan are its cult wines.","fact":"Napa Valley's signature is Cabernet Sauvignon."},{"id":"WN-106","lv":"nw","d":4,"area":"The New World","q":"Opus One is a joint venture of Mondavi and","o":["Antinori","Mouton Rothschild","Lafite Rothschild","Penfolds"],"a":[1],"s":1,"x":"A Bordeaux blend made in Napa.","fact":"Opus One was founded in 1979 by Robert Mondavi and Baron Philippe de Rothschild of Mouton."},{"id":"WN-107","lv":"nw","d":3,"area":"The New World","q":"Oregon's Willamette Valley is known for","o":["Zinfandel","Malbec","Cabernet Sauvignon","Pinot Noir"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"Same latitude as Burgundy, near enough.","fact":"Oregon's Willamette Valley is America's Pinot Noir heartland; Burgundy's Drouhin family bought land there in 1987."},{"id":"WN-108","lv":"nw","d":3,"area":"The New World","q":"Penfolds Grange is mostly","o":["Grenache","Cabernet Sauvignon","Pinot Noir","Shiraz"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"Inspired by Schubert's visit to Bordeaux.","fact":"Penfolds Grange, first made by Max Schubert in 1951, is Australia's most famous wine, mostly Shiraz."},{"id":"WN-109","lv":"nw","d":4,"area":"The New World","q":"The Barossa Valley is famous for","o":["sparkling wine","Sauvignon Blanc","old-vine Shiraz","Pinot Noir"],"a":[2],"s":1,"x":"Settled by Silesian Lutherans, hence the German names.","fact":"The Barossa holds some of the world's oldest Shiraz vines, planted in the 1840s and spared by phylloxera."},{"id":"WN-110","lv":"nw","d":5,"area":"The New World","q":"Coonawarra's famous soil is","o":["black cotton soil over basalt","grey slate over sandstone","white chalk over flint","terra rossa over limestone"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"A cigar-shaped strip about 15 km long.","fact":"Coonawarra's terra rossa, red earth over limestone, makes Australia's classic Cabernet."},{"id":"WN-111","lv":"nw","d":5,"area":"The New World","q":"The Hunter Valley is famous for a long-lived, unoaked","o":["Viognier","Sémillon","Chardonnay","Riesling"],"a":[1],"s":1,"x":"Lean and lemony young; it looks oaked after ten years in bottle.","fact":"Hunter Valley Sémillon is picked early at 10–11% alcohol and ages into toast and honey without oak."},{"id":"WN-112","lv":"nw","d":2,"area":"The New World","q":"Cloudy Bay made its name with","o":["Chardonnay","Riesling","Pinot Noir","Sauvignon Blanc"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"Today it's owned by LVMH.","fact":"Marlborough Sauvignon Blanc, put on the map by Cloudy Bay's 1985 vintage, is New Zealand's calling card."},{"id":"WN-113","lv":"nw","d":4,"area":"The New World","q":"Central Otago is known for","o":["Pinot Noir","Shiraz","Malbec","Sauvignon Blanc"],"a":[0],"s":1,"x":"Continental, sunny and cold at night.","fact":"Central Otago, among the world's southernmost wine regions, makes New Zealand's best Pinot Noir."},{"id":"WN-114","lv":"nw","d":5,"area":"The New World","q":"Until 1994, Chile's Carménère was thought to be","o":["Malbec","Merlot","Cabernet Franc","Syrah"],"a":[1],"s":1,"x":"A Bordeaux grape all but lost in France after phylloxera.","fact":"Chile's Carménère was mistaken for Merlot until 1994; and Chile has never had phylloxera."},{"id":"WN-115","lv":"nw","d":5,"area":"The New World","q":"Pinotage is a cross of Pinot Noir and","o":["Cinsault","Syrah","Merlot","Gamay"],"a":[0],"s":1,"x":"Bred by Abraham Perold at Stellenbosch.","fact":"Pinotage is South Africa's own grape: Pinot Noir crossed with Cinsault (then called Hermitage) in 1925."},{"id":"WN-116","lv":"nw","d":6,"area":"The New World","q":"Napoleon drank in exile a sweet wine from","o":["Constantia","Tokaj","Sauternes","Madeira"],"a":[0],"s":1,"x":"Jane Austen mentions it too.","fact":"Vin de Constance, from Constantia near Cape Town, was Napoleon's comfort in exile on Saint Helena."},{"id":"WN-117","lv":"nw","d":3,"area":"The New World","q":"Mendoza's vineyards grow","o":["in rainforest","at sea level on the Atlantic","on volcanic islands","high in the Andes' rain shadow"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"Irrigated with snowmelt; the Uco Valley reaches about 1,500 metres.","fact":"Mendoza's vineyards sit high in the rain shadow of the Andes: strong sun, cold nights."},{"id":"WN-118","lv":"nw","d":4,"area":"The New World","q":"Château Musar is from","o":["Israel","Morocco","Greece","Lebanon"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"Its long-lived reds blend Cabernet, Cinsault and Carignan.","fact":"Château Musar, from Lebanon's Bekaa Valley, kept making wine through the civil war."},{"id":"WN-119","lv":"nw","d":4,"area":"The New World","q":"Georgian qvevri are","o":["stone troughs for treading grapes","goatskin bags for carrying wine","clay jars buried in the ground","oak casks sunk in cold streams"],"a":[2],"s":1,"x":"On UNESCO's intangible heritage list since 2013.","fact":"Georgia has the oldest evidence of winemaking, about 8,000 years, and still ferments in clay jars buried in the ground: qvevri."},{"id":"WN-120","lv":"nw","d":5,"area":"The New World","q":"China's best-known fine-wine region is","o":["Hainan","Guangdong","Ningxia","Heilongjiang"],"a":[2],"s":1,"x":"China has one of the largest vineyard areas in the world, much of it for table grapes.","fact":"China's Ningxia, at the foot of the Helan Mountains, is its rising fine-wine region; Changyu, founded in 1892, its oldest producer."},{"id":"WN-121","lv":"mk","d":1,"area":"Making wine","q":"Fermentation turns sugar into","o":["alcohol and carbon dioxide","alcohol and oxygen","acid and carbon dioxide","glycerol and water"],"a":[0],"s":1,"x":"Stop it early and sugar remains: sweetness.","fact":"Yeast turns grape sugar into alcohol and carbon dioxide."},{"id":"WN-122","lv":"mk","d":2,"area":"Making wine","q":"Red wine gets its colour from","o":["added colouring","the skins","the juice","the barrel"],"a":[1],"s":1,"x":"Champagne's Blanc de Noirs is white wine from black grapes.","fact":"Almost all grape juice is clear: a red wine's colour comes from the skins, macerated with the juice."},{"id":"WN-123","lv":"mk","d":4,"area":"Making wine","q":"In the EU, blending red and white wine into rosé is allowed only for","o":["all rosés","Provence rosé","Tavel","rosé Champagne"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"Everywhere else, rosé must be made from the skins.","fact":"Rosé takes its colour from a few hours on the skins; in the EU, blending red and white is allowed only for rosé Champagne."},{"id":"WN-124","lv":"mk","d":3,"area":"Making wine","q":"The buttery note in some Chardonnay comes from","o":["botrytis","added butter flavour","malolactic conversion","oak alone"],"a":[2],"s":1,"x":"The compound is diacetyl; most reds go through it too.","fact":"Malolactic conversion turns sharp malic acid into softer lactic acid; it gives Chardonnay its butter."},{"id":"WN-125","lv":"mk","d":3,"area":"Making wine","q":"A Bordeaux barrique holds","o":["100 litres","500 litres","1,000 litres","225 litres"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"French oak is subtler; American oak gives coconut and dill.","fact":"New oak adds vanilla, toast and spice; a Bordeaux barrique holds 225 litres, about 300 bottles."},{"id":"WN-126","lv":"mk","d":3,"area":"Making wine","q":"Which of these adds no tannin?","o":["grape seeds and stems","malolactic conversion","new oak barrels","grape skins"],"a":[1],"s":1,"x":"Tannin softens with age and with protein in food.","fact":"Tannin comes from skins, seeds and stems, and from oak; it dries the mouth like strong tea."},{"id":"WN-127","lv":"mk","d":3,"area":"Making wine","q":"Carbonic maceration is used most in","o":["Sauternes","Barolo","Champagne","Beaujolais"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"The fermentation starts inside each intact berry.","fact":"Carbonic maceration ferments whole bunches inside the berries under CO2: fruity, low in tannin, banana and bubblegum."},{"id":"WN-128","lv":"mk","d":4,"area":"Making wine","q":"Tokaji Aszú, Hungary's sweet wine, is made from","o":["frozen grapes","botrytised grapes","grapes dried on mats","fortified must"],"a":[1],"s":1,"x":"Its sweetness was once counted in puttonyos, baskets of aszú berries.","fact":"Botrytis cinerea, noble rot, shrivels the grapes and concentrates sugar: Sauternes, Tokaji Aszú, Trockenbeerenauslese."},{"id":"WN-129","lv":"mk","d":4,"area":"Making wine","q":"Orange wine is","o":["red grapes pressed off their skins at once","white wine flavoured with orange peel","rosé aged in old orange-wood casks","white grapes fermented on their skins"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"Also called amber or skin-contact wine.","fact":"Orange wine is white wine made like red, fermented on the skins: amber and tannic, from Georgia and Friuli."},{"id":"WN-130","lv":"mk","d":3,"area":"Making wine","q":"Europe's vineyards survived phylloxera by","o":["releasing ladybirds to eat the aphids","grafting onto American rootstock","spraying the vines with copper sulphate","breeding resistant European vines"],"a":[1],"s":1,"x":"A few ungrafted vineyards survive, on sand or in Chile.","fact":"Phylloxera, an American aphid, destroyed Europe's vineyards from the 1860s; vines are now grafted onto American rootstock."},{"id":"WN-131","lv":"mk","d":2,"area":"Making wine","q":"High acid, low alcohol and green fruit most likely mean","o":["a hot climate","late-harvest grapes","a cool climate","a fortified wine"],"a":[2],"s":1,"x":"Sugar becomes alcohol; heat burns off acid.","fact":"Cool climates give high acidity and lower alcohol; warm climates give riper fruit, softer acid and more alcohol."},{"id":"WN-132","lv":"mk","d":2,"area":"Making wine","q":"A label naming the grape rather than the place is typical of","o":["Bordeaux","the New World","Rioja","Burgundy"],"a":[1],"s":1,"x":"Alsace is the French exception.","fact":"Old World labels name the place; New World labels name the grape."},{"id":"WN-133","lv":"mk","d":3,"area":"Making wine","q":"Sulphites are added to wine","o":["to sweeten it and round off its acidity","to keep the bubbles in sparkling wine","to deepen its colour as it ages","to protect it from oxidation and microbes"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"Dried apricots contain far more.","fact":"Sulphur dioxide is added to protect wine from oxidation and microbes; 'contains sulphites' must be on the label."},{"id":"WN-134","lv":"mk","d":4,"area":"Making wine","q":"'Vieilles Vignes' on a French label means","o":["an old cellar, of at least a century","aged in old barrels for two years","old vines, with no legal definition","old vines, legally over fifty years old"],"a":[2],"s":1,"x":"Some producers use it for vines of 30 years, others for 80.","fact":"'Vieilles Vignes' (old vines) is unregulated in France: old vines give lower yields and more concentration."},{"id":"WN-135","lv":"sv","d":2,"area":"Service, pairing & faults","q":"The best serving temperature for a full-bodied red","o":["6–8 °C","22–24 °C","10–12 °C","15–18 °C"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"'Room temperature' meant a cold European dining room.","fact":"Full reds are best at 15–18 °C, light reds a little cooler, whites 8–12 °C, sparkling 6–10 °C."},{"id":"WN-136","lv":"sv","d":3,"area":"Service, pairing & faults","q":"Which wine most needs decanting off its sediment?","o":["Muscadet","Vintage Port","Beaujolais Nouveau","Prosecco"],"a":[1],"s":1,"x":"Stand the bottle upright a day before.","fact":"Decant to leave an old wine's sediment behind, or to give a young tannic red some air."},{"id":"WN-137","lv":"sv","d":2,"area":"Service, pairing & faults","q":"The sommelier pours you a taste to check","o":["whether you like the wine you ordered","that the vintage matches the list","that the wine is at the right temperature","for faults such as cork taint"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"Disliking a sound wine isn't grounds to send it back.","fact":"In a restaurant, the taste you're poured is to check the wine is sound, not whether you like it."},{"id":"WN-138","lv":"sv","d":3,"area":"Service, pairing & faults","q":"Cork taint smells of","o":["vinegar and sharp acid","musty, wet cardboard","rotten eggs and struck matches","nail polish and glue"],"a":[1],"s":1,"x":"2,4,6-trichloroanisole: detectable at a few parts per trillion.","fact":"Cork taint (TCA) smells of musty, wet cardboard and mutes the fruit; it's a fault, so the bottle can go back."},{"id":"WN-139","lv":"sv","d":3,"area":"Service, pairing & faults","q":"A brown, sherry-like table wine is probably","o":["reduced","too young","corked","oxidised"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"Fine in sherry, a fault in a young white.","fact":"Oxidised wine turns brown and smells of bruised apple or sherry; reduction smells of struck matches."},{"id":"WN-140","lv":"sv","d":4,"area":"Service, pairing & faults","q":"A 'barnyard' smell in red wine comes from","o":["Brettanomyces yeast","too much sulphur","cork taint (TCA)","new oak barrels"],"a":[0],"s":1,"x":"Some old Bordeaux and Rhône reds wear it proudly.","fact":"Brettanomyces, a yeast, gives barnyard and sticking-plaster notes: a fault, though a little is tolerated in some reds."},{"id":"WN-141","lv":"sv","d":2,"area":"Service, pairing & faults","q":"The classic match for a tannic Cabernet Sauvignon","o":["oysters","chocolate mousse","sushi","grilled steak"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"Protein binds the tannin; the wine tastes rounder.","fact":"Tannic reds suit fat and protein: steak with Cabernet softens the tannin."},{"id":"WN-142","lv":"sv","d":3,"area":"Service, pairing & faults","q":"With grilled salmon, the safer red is","o":["Amarone","Pinot Noir","Barolo","Cabernet Sauvignon"],"a":[1],"s":1,"x":"Rosé works too.","fact":"Tannin with oily fish can taste metallic; with salmon choose a white or a light, low-tannin red such as Pinot Noir."},{"id":"WN-143","lv":"sv","d":3,"area":"Service, pairing & faults","q":"With a sweet dessert, the wine should be","o":["dry and sparkling, to cleanse the palate","tannic and red, to balance it","at least as sweet as the dessert","drier than the dessert, to cut it"],"a":[2],"s":1,"x":"Dry Champagne with wedding cake is the classic mistake.","fact":"A dessert wine should be at least as sweet as the dessert, or the wine tastes thin and sour."},{"id":"WN-144","lv":"sv","d":3,"area":"Service, pairing & faults","q":"The classic match for Stilton","o":["Chablis","Beaujolais","Port","Champagne"],"a":[2],"s":1,"x":"Blue cheese's salt makes sweet wine taste fresher.","fact":"Salt loves sweetness: Roquefort with Sauternes, Stilton with Port."},{"id":"WN-145","lv":"sv","d":3,"area":"Service, pairing & faults","q":"With a spicy Thai curry, the best choice is","o":["a 15% Zinfandel","a tannic Barolo","an off-dry Riesling","an oaky Chardonnay"],"a":[2],"s":1,"x":"Alcohol and tannin amplify the burn.","fact":"Spicy food wants low alcohol, low tannin and a touch of sweetness: off-dry Riesling or Gewürztraminer."},{"id":"WN-146","lv":"sv","d":4,"area":"Service, pairing & faults","q":"Crottin de Chavignol goat's cheese classically goes with","o":["Barolo","Sancerre","Port","Rioja"],"a":[1],"s":1,"x":"Chavignol is a village of the Sancerre appellation.","fact":"What grows together goes together: Chablis with oysters, Sancerre with Crottin de Chavignol goat's cheese."},{"id":"WN-147","lv":"sv","d":2,"area":"Service, pairing & faults","q":"The traditional wine with a Swiss fondue","o":["a dry white such as Chasselas","a sweet white such as Malvoisie","a light red such as Pinot Noir","a sparkling rosé such as Mauler"],"a":[0],"s":1,"x":"Or black tea; never cold water, says tradition.","fact":"Fondue and raclette go with a dry white: Fendant (Chasselas) is the Valais's own answer."},{"id":"WN-148","lv":"sv","d":4,"area":"Service, pairing & faults","q":"The high shoulders of a Bordeaux bottle are said to","o":["hold a little more wine per bottle","catch sediment when pouring","stand more firmly in a rack","keep the wine cooler in the cellar"],"a":[1],"s":1,"x":"The Rhône uses the Burgundy shape; Alsace and Germany the tall flute.","fact":"Bordeaux bottles have high shoulders, said to catch sediment when pouring; Burgundy bottles slope."},{"id":"WN-149","lv":"tr","d":4,"area":"The trade","q":"Buying Bordeaux en primeur means","o":["paying now for wine delivered in about two years","paying on delivery for wine bottled two years ago","buying the first bottles of a vintage on release","buying at the château's own auction each spring"],"a":[0],"s":1,"x":"Released in tranches at prices set by each château.","fact":"Bordeaux is sold en primeur: as futures, the spring after the harvest, for delivery about two years later."},{"id":"WN-150","lv":"tr","d":5,"area":"The trade","q":"On the Place de Bordeaux, a courtier is","o":["a grower who sells only to merchants","a broker between château and merchant","a merchant who sells to the world","a sommelier who tastes for the châteaux"],"a":[1],"s":1,"x":"A three-tier market older than most commodity exchanges.","fact":"The Place de Bordeaux: châteaux sell through courtiers (brokers) to négociants (merchants), who sell to the world."},{"id":"WN-151","lv":"tr","d":4,"area":"The trade","q":"Liv-ex is","o":["a fine-wine exchange in London","a Bordeaux château","a wine tax","an auction house in Hong Kong"],"a":[0],"s":1,"x":"Prices are quoted per case, in bond.","fact":"Liv-ex, the London International Vintners Exchange (2000), is the fine-wine trading platform; its Fine Wine 100 is the market's benchmark index."},{"id":"WN-152","lv":"tr","d":4,"area":"The trade","q":"Wine 'in bond' has","o":["duty and VAT not yet paid","a broken seal","no label","been bottled at the château"],"a":[0],"s":1,"x":"Duty falls due only when it leaves for drinking.","fact":"Wine held 'in bond' sits in a bonded warehouse with duty and VAT unpaid: it's how fine wine is traded."},{"id":"WN-153","lv":"tr","d":4,"area":"The trade","q":"OWC stands for","o":["original wooden case","one-way container","old wine certified","official wine classification"],"a":[0],"s":1,"x":"Provenance is to fine wine what a certificate of origin is to a cargo.","fact":"OWC, the original wooden case, and provenance add value: a case kept in bond in its OWC trades at a premium."},{"id":"WN-154","lv":"tr","d":4,"area":"The trade","q":"Robert Parker made his name calling the Bordeaux vintage of","o":["1982","2000","1990","1961"],"a":[0],"s":1,"x":"Critics thought 1982 too ripe; he called it great, and was right.","fact":"Robert Parker's 100-point scores, from 1978, moved Bordeaux prices; his call on the 1982 vintage made his name."},{"id":"WN-155","lv":"tr","d":5,"area":"The trade","q":"From the 2008 vintage, Lafite engraved on its bottles","o":["the Chinese character for eight","the Chinese character for luck","a red dragon for the Year of the Rat","a gold seal of the emperor"],"a":[0],"s":1,"x":"The textbook fine-wine bubble.","fact":"Chinese demand sent Lafite's prices soaring from 2009 to 2011, then crashing; from the 2008 vintage, Lafite engraved the lucky 8 on its bottles."},{"id":"WN-156","lv":"tr","d":5,"area":"The trade","q":"Why did Hong Kong become a wine-auction hub after 2008?","o":["it banned imports","it planted vineyards","it abolished wine duty","it hosted the Hospices auction"],"a":[2],"s":1,"x":"Overnight, it outgrew New York as the top auction market.","fact":"Hong Kong scrapped its wine duty in 2008 and became one of the world's biggest wine-auction centres."},{"id":"WN-157","lv":"tr","d":5,"area":"The trade","q":"Rudy Kurniawan was convicted of","o":["evading tax on Champagne","smuggling Port","faking fine wine","stealing a château"],"a":[2],"s":1,"x":"The case that made provenance an obsession.","fact":"Rudy Kurniawan, convicted in 2013, made fake Burgundy in his kitchen and sold it at auction for millions."},{"id":"WN-158","lv":"tr","d":3,"area":"The trade","q":"A case of wine holds","o":["12 litres (16 bottles)","6 litres (8 bottles)","9 litres (12 bottles)","7.5 litres (10 bottles)"],"a":[2],"s":1,"x":"Fine wine also trades in cases of 6, and magnums.","fact":"A standard bottle is 75 cl; a case is 12 bottles, 9 litres; a barrique of 225 litres fills about 300 bottles."},{"id":"WN-159","lv":"tr","d":5,"area":"The trade","q":"Which country has the largest vineyard area in the world?","o":["Spain","France","the USA","Italy"],"a":[0],"s":1,"x":"Spanish yields are low, so it ranks third by volume.","fact":"Italy and France take turns as the largest wine producer by volume; Spain has the largest vineyard area."},{"id":"WN-160","lv":"tr","d":6,"area":"The trade","q":"Under the RED's Annex IX, grape marc and wine lees are","o":["excluded as food crops","not listed","Part B, with used cooking oil","Part A, advanced feedstocks"],"a":[3],"s":1,"x":"Italy's distilleries turn them into advanced bioethanol.","fact":"Grape marc and wine lees are listed in Part A of Annex IX of the Renewable Energy Directive: feedstock for advanced biofuels."},{"id":"WN-161","lv":"tr","d":5,"area":"The trade","q":"EU crisis distillation turns surplus wine into","o":["vinegar for the food industry","grape juice for schools","industrial alcohol such as ethanol","brandy for export to Asia"],"a":[2],"s":1,"x":"The 'wine lake' of the 1980s went the same way.","fact":"The EU's crisis distillation turns surplus wine into industrial alcohol: in 2020, French wine became hand sanitiser and fuel ethanol."},{"id":"WN-162","lv":"tr","d":5,"area":"The trade","q":"Which is NOT a great Bordeaux vintage?","o":["2005","2010","2013","2016"],"a":[2],"s":1,"x":"2013 was wet, cold and rotten: the weakest of the decade.","fact":"The great Bordeaux vintages this century: 2000, 2005, 2009, 2010, 2015, 2016, 2019, 2020."}];
